/* ===== System design topics B: data, partitioning, messaging ===== */

SDX('sqlnosql', { name: 'SQL vs NoSQL', cat: 'sddata',
  blurb: `Relational databases give you tables, joins and transactions. NoSQL databases (key-value, document, wide-column, graph) give up some of that for easier horizontal scaling, flexible schemas or a data model that fits one access pattern perfectly. Large systems use several, each for what it does best.`,
  facts: [['Relational', 'PostgreSQL, MySQL'], ['Key-value', 'Redis, DynamoDB'], ['Document', 'MongoDB'], ['Wide-column', 'Cassandra, HBase']],
  dg: { w: 780, h: 360,
    n: [['app', 'Application', 110, 180, 'svc'], ['sql', 'PostgreSQL', 470, 40, 'db', 'relational'], ['kv', 'Redis', 650, 115, 'db', 'key-value'], ['doc', 'MongoDB', 650, 245, 'db', 'document'], ['wc', 'Cassandra', 470, 320, 'db', 'wide-column'], ['gr', 'Neo4j', 300, 320, 'db', 'graph']],
    e: [['app', 'sql', 'orders, payments'], ['app', 'kv', 'sessions, counters'], ['app', 'doc', 'product catalog'], ['app', 'wc', 'activity events'], ['app', 'gr', 'social graph']] },
  cap: `Polyglot persistence: each kind of data goes to the store whose model and guarantees fit it. Money needs transactions; sessions need speed; events need cheap, massive writes.`,
  walk: [[['app', 'sql', 'app>sql'], `Orders and payments need multi-row ACID transactions, constraints and ad-hoc queries with joins: a relational database.`],
    [['app', 'kv', 'app>kv'], `Sessions, rate-limit counters and caches are looked up by one key, millions of times a second: an in-memory key-value store.`],
    [['app', 'doc', 'app>doc'], `Product listings have varied, nested attributes (a shoe has sizes, a laptop has CPU options) and are read as a whole: a document store fits naturally.`],
    [['app', 'wc', 'app>wc'], `Billions of activity events, written constantly and read by user and time range: a wide-column store spreads them across many nodes with fast sequential writes.`],
    [['app', 'gr', 'app>gr'], `“Friends of friends who like X” needs many hops through relationships, which a graph database answers without expensive self-joins.`]],
  sections: [
    ['The families', TBL(['Type', 'Data model', 'Strengths', 'Limits', 'Examples'], [['Relational', 'Tables with fixed schemas, foreign keys', 'Joins, ACID transactions, mature SQL, constraints', 'Scaling writes past one machine needs sharding', 'PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server'], ['Key-value', 'key → opaque value', 'Fastest lookups, simple to partition', 'No queries by value', 'Redis, DynamoDB, etcd'], ['Document', 'JSON-like documents', 'Flexible schema, whole-object reads', 'Weak joins; denormalized data duplicates', 'MongoDB, Couchbase, Firestore'], ['Wide-column', 'Rows with partition key + sorted columns', 'Huge write throughput, linear scaling, multi-datacenter', 'Must design tables per query; limited ad-hoc querying', 'Cassandra, HBase, ScyllaDB, Bigtable'], ['Graph', 'Nodes and edges', 'Multi-hop relationship queries', 'Harder to scale out', 'Neo4j, Neptune'], ['Time-series', 'Timestamped points', 'Compression, downsampling, time windows', 'Narrow use', 'InfluxDB, TimescaleDB, Prometheus'], ['NewSQL / distributed SQL', 'Relational, sharded automatically', 'SQL + transactions + horizontal scale', 'Higher latency per write, complexity', 'Spanner, CockroachDB, YugabyteDB']])],
    ['How to choose', OL([`Start from access patterns: which queries, how often, how much data, what latency.`, `Need transactions across rows or complex queries? Relational (or distributed SQL at large scale).`, `Simple lookups by key at very high throughput? Key-value.`, `Self-contained aggregates with varying shape? Document.`, `Massive append-heavy data queried by key and time? Wide-column or time-series.`, `Default to PostgreSQL when unsure: it handles far more scale than most products ever reach, and supports JSON columns too.`])],
    ['Modeling differences', PP(`Relational modeling normalizes data (each fact stored once, joined at read time). NoSQL modeling usually denormalizes: you design one table or document per query, duplicating data so each read touches one partition. Writes then update several copies, often asynchronously. In Cassandra you might keep both messages_by_conversation and messages_by_user tables with the same data.`)],
    ['Myths to avoid', UL([`“SQL doesn’t scale.” Read replicas, partitioning and careful indexing take relational databases a very long way; sharded MySQL runs some of the largest sites in the world.`, `“NoSQL means no schema.” The schema still exists, in your application code; it is just not enforced by the database.`, `“NoSQL is always eventually consistent.” Many offer tunable or strong consistency (DynamoDB strongly consistent reads, MongoDB majority writes).`])]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('Which store best fits a bank’s account balances and transfers?', ['A key-value cache', 'A relational database with ACID transactions', 'A graph database', 'A CDN'], 1, 'Transfers must update two balances atomically with constraints: classic relational territory.'),
    SQ('Why do Cassandra users often store the same data in several tables?', ['To back it up', 'Each table is designed for one query, so reads hit a single partition', 'Cassandra can’t store large rows', 'Joins are slow in SQL'], 1, 'Query-first, denormalized modeling trades write work and storage for fast single-partition reads.')]
});

SDX('indexes', { name: 'Indexes: B-trees & LSM trees', cat: 'sddata',
  blurb: `Without an index a database must scan every row to answer a query. An index is a separate sorted structure that finds rows in O(log n). B-trees are optimized for reads and updates in place; LSM trees for very high write throughput.`,
  facts: [['Lookup without index', 'O(n) scan'], ['B-tree lookup', 'O(log n), ≈ 3–4 page reads'], ['Cost', 'Slower writes, more storage'], ['LSM users', 'Cassandra, RocksDB']],
  dg: { w: 780, h: 290,
    n: [['r', '50', 390, 40, 'box', 'root'], ['i1', '20 | 35', 200, 130, 'box'], ['i2', '70 | 85', 580, 130, 'box'],
      ['l1', '10 · 15', 70, 240, 'box'], ['l2', '20 · 28', 195, 240, 'box'], ['l3', '35 · 42', 320, 240, 'box'], ['l4', '50 · 61', 455, 240, 'box'], ['l5', '70 · 77', 580, 240, 'box'], ['l6', '85 · 93', 705, 240, 'box']],
    e: [['r', 'i1', '< 50'], ['r', 'i2', '≥ 50'], ['i1', 'l1'], ['i1', 'l2'], ['i1', 'l3', '≥ 35'], ['i2', 'l4'], ['i2', 'l5'], ['i2', 'l6'], ['l1', 'l2', '', 'd'], ['l2', 'l3', '', 'd'], ['l3', 'l4', '', 'd'], ['l4', 'l5', '', 'd'], ['l5', 'l6', '', 'd']] },
  cap: `A B-tree index: each node is a disk page holding sorted keys. A lookup follows one path from root to leaf; linked leaves make range scans sequential.`,
  walk: [[['r'], `Find key 42. Start at the root page, which says keys below 50 are in the left subtree.`],
    [['r', 'i1', 'r>i1'], `Read the internal page [20 | 35]. 42 ≥ 35, so take the rightmost child.`],
    [['i1', 'l3', 'i1>l3'], `The leaf page holds 35 and 42, each with a pointer to the row (or the row itself in a clustered index). Three page reads in total.`],
    [['l3', 'l4', 'l5', 'l3>l4', 'l4>l5'], `A range query such as 40 ≤ key ≤ 75 finds the first leaf, then walks the sibling links in order without returning to the root.`],
    [['r', 'i1', 'i2'], `Real B-trees have hundreds of keys per 8–16 KB page, so a table with a billion rows needs only 3–4 levels, and the top levels stay in memory.`]],
  dg2: { w: 780, h: 250,
    n: [['w', 'Writes', 80, 70, 'client'], ['mt', 'Memtable', 300, 70, 'cache', 'sorted, in memory'], ['wal', 'Write-ahead log', 300, 190, 'store', 'append-only'], ['l0', 'SSTables L0', 540, 70, 'db', 'immutable files'], ['l1', 'SSTables L1', 710, 190, 'db', 'merged']],
    e: [['w', 'mt', 'insert'], ['w', 'wal', 'append first'], ['mt', 'l0', 'flush when full'], ['l0', 'l1', 'compaction']] },
  dg2t: 'Compare: LSM tree', cap2: `An LSM tree turns random writes into sequential ones: writes go to a log and an in-memory table, which is flushed as sorted immutable files that background compaction merges.`,
  sections: [
    ['Why indexes matter', PP(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ? on 100 million rows scans every row without an index: seconds of I/O. With a B-tree index on email it reads about four pages: under a millisecond if cached. Most slow-query fixes are a missing or wrong index.`)],
    ['B-trees', UL([`Balanced trees of fixed-size pages; every leaf is at the same depth, so lookups are predictably O(log n).`, `Updates modify pages in place, protected by a write-ahead log for crash safety.`, `Excellent for reads, point lookups and range scans; the default index in PostgreSQL, MySQL (InnoDB) and most relational databases.`, `Random writes cause random page updates, which limits write throughput.`])],
    ['LSM trees', UL([`Writes append to a log and go into a sorted in-memory table (memtable); full memtables are flushed to disk as immutable sorted files (SSTables).`, `Background compaction merges files and discards overwritten or deleted values (tombstones).`, `Reads may check several files; Bloom filters per file skip files that can’t contain the key.`, `Very high write throughput and good compression; used by Cassandra, RocksDB, LevelDB, HBase and ScyllaDB.`])],
    ['Kinds of index', TBL(['Index', 'What it does'], [['Primary / clustered', 'Table rows stored in key order (InnoDB primary key)'], ['Secondary', 'Separate structure pointing to rows'], ['Composite', 'Several columns, e.g. (user_id, created_at): serves queries on user_id, and user_id + time range, but not created_at alone'], ['Covering', 'Includes all columns a query needs, so the table is never read'], ['Unique', 'Also enforces uniqueness'], ['Hash', 'O(1) equality lookups, no ranges'], ['Full-text / inverted', 'Word → documents, for search'], ['Geospatial', 'R-trees, geohashes for “near me” queries']])],
    ['Costs and tips', UL([`Each index slows every insert and update and uses storage. Index for the queries you actually run.`, `Put the equality column first and the range column last in composite indexes.`, `Low-selectivity columns (a boolean) rarely benefit from their own index.`, `Use EXPLAIN to see whether a query uses an index or scans.`])]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('A composite index on (user_id, created_at) helps which query?', ['WHERE created_at > yesterday', 'WHERE user_id = 7 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20', 'WHERE email = ?', 'WHERE created_at = ? AND email = ?'], 1, 'The index is sorted by user_id, then created_at within each user, exactly what this query reads.'),
    SQ('Why do LSM trees handle writes faster than B-trees?', ['They skip durability', 'They convert random writes into sequential appends and batch flushes', 'They have no indexes', 'They store data only in memory'], 1, 'Sequential I/O is far cheaper than random page updates; compaction happens in the background.')]
});

SDX('transactions', { name: 'Transactions, ACID & isolation', cat: 'sddata',
  blurb: `A transaction groups several reads and writes so they succeed or fail as one unit. ACID describes the guarantees; isolation levels describe how much concurrent transactions can see of each other, trading safety for performance.`,
  facts: [['A', 'Atomicity: all or nothing'], ['C', 'Consistency: invariants hold'], ['I', 'Isolation: as if alone'], ['D', 'Durability: survives crashes']],
  dg: { w: 760, h: 300,
    n: [['app', 'App', 90, 150, 'svc'], ['db', 'Database engine', 370, 150, 'svc', 'locks · MVCC'], ['wal', 'Write-ahead log', 640, 60, 'store', 'fsync on commit'], ['tbl', 'Accounts table', 640, 245, 'db', 'pages updated later']],
    e: [['app', 'db', 'BEGIN … COMMIT', 'u'], ['db', 'app', 'OK / ROLLBACK', 'u', 'db>app'], ['db', 'wal', 'append records'], ['db', 'tbl', 'apply to pages']] },
  cap: `A transfer of $100 from A to B. The commit is durable once its log record is on disk; data pages can be written later because the log can replay them after a crash.`,
  walk: [[['app', 'db', 'app>db'], `BEGIN; UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance − 100 WHERE id = 'A'; UPDATE … + 100 WHERE id = 'B'; COMMIT.`],
    [['db'], `The engine locks or versions the two rows so concurrent transactions see either the before state or the after state, never half of it (isolation).`],
    [['db', 'wal', 'db>wal'], `On COMMIT it appends the changes to the write-ahead log and forces it to disk (fsync). Only then does it acknowledge. That is durability.`],
    [['db', 'app', 'db>app'], `If anything fails before the commit record is written, recovery discards the partial work: atomicity. The app sees either success or a clean rollback.`],
    [['db', 'tbl', 'db>tbl'], `Changed pages are written to the table files later, in the background. After a crash the log is replayed to bring pages up to date.`]],
  sections: [
    ['ACID in plain words', TBL(['Property', 'Meaning', 'How databases provide it'], [['Atomicity', 'All of the transaction happens, or none of it', 'Undo/redo logs, rollback'], ['Consistency', 'Data moves from one valid state to another (constraints, invariants)', 'Constraints, plus correct application logic'], ['Isolation', 'Concurrent transactions don’t see each other’s partial work', 'Locks, MVCC snapshots'], ['Durability', 'Committed data survives crashes', 'Write-ahead log flushed to disk, replication']])],
    ['Isolation levels and anomalies', TBL(['Level', 'Prevents', 'Still allows'], [['Read uncommitted', 'Nothing much', 'Dirty reads of uncommitted data'], ['Read committed', 'Dirty reads', 'Non-repeatable reads: a row changes between two reads'], ['Repeatable read / snapshot', 'Non-repeatable reads', 'Write skew (two transactions each check a condition, then both write)'], ['Serializable', 'All anomalies: result equals some serial order', 'Nothing, at a performance cost (retries, locking)']]) +
      PP(`PostgreSQL defaults to read committed, MySQL InnoDB to repeatable read. Classic bugs: two users booking the last seat (write skew), or lost updates from read–modify–write without locking. Fix with SELECT … FOR UPDATE, atomic updates (SET stock = stock − 1 WHERE stock &gt; 0), optimistic version checks, or serializable isolation.`)],
    ['MVCC', PP(`Multi-version concurrency control keeps several versions of each row. Readers see a consistent snapshot as of their start time and never block writers; writers create new versions. Old versions are cleaned up later (VACUUM in PostgreSQL). It is why reads in modern databases rarely wait on locks.`)],
    ['Transactions in distributed systems', PP(`Inside one database, transactions are cheap and well understood. Across services or shards they are hard: you need two-phase commit, or you redesign around sagas, idempotent steps and eventual consistency. Many designs keep data that must change together in the same database (or the same shard) precisely to keep local transactions.`)]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('Two transactions both read stock = 1 and both write stock = 0, selling the item twice. Which fix works?', ['Increase the cache TTL', 'UPDATE items SET stock = stock − 1 WHERE id = ? AND stock > 0, and check rows affected', 'Use read uncommitted', 'Add a secondary index'], 1, 'The conditional atomic update lets only one transaction succeed.')]
});

SDX('replication', { name: 'Replication', cat: 'sddist',
  blurb: `Replication keeps copies of the same data on several machines, for availability (survive a failure), read scaling (serve reads from replicas) and lower latency (copies near users). The big choices are who accepts writes and whether to wait for replicas before confirming them.`,
  facts: [['Single-leader', 'Most common'], ['Sync replica', 'No data loss, slower writes'], ['Async replica', 'Fast, can lag'], ['Typical lag', 'ms to seconds']],
  dg: { w: 760, h: 320,
    n: [['app', 'App servers', 100, 160, 'svc'], ['L', 'Leader', 380, 70, 'db', 'accepts writes'], ['f1', 'Follower 1', 640, 70, 'db', 'sync · same region'], ['f2', 'Follower 2', 640, 250, 'db', 'async · other region'], ['f3', 'Follower 3', 380, 250, 'db', 'async · reads']],
    e: [['app', 'L', 'writes'], ['app', 'f3', 'reads', 'd'], ['L', 'f1', 'wait for ack'], ['L', 'f2', 'stream log (lag)', 'd'], ['L', 'f3', 'stream log', 'd']] },
  cap: `Single-leader replication: all writes go to the leader, which streams its log to followers. One synchronous follower guarantees a second copy of every commit; asynchronous followers serve reads and remote regions.`,
  walk: [[['app', 'L', 'app>L'], `Every write goes to the leader, which orders all changes in its write-ahead log.`],
    [['L', 'f1', 'L>f1'], `The leader waits until the synchronous follower confirms it has the change before acknowledging the client. If the leader dies, no acknowledged write is lost.`],
    [['L', 'f2', 'f3', 'L>f2', 'L>f3'], `Asynchronous followers apply the stream a little later. They don’t slow writes, but they may lag by milliseconds or, under load, seconds.`],
    [['app', 'f3', 'app>f3'], `Reads are spread across followers, multiplying read capacity. The catch: a user may write then read from a lagging follower and not see their own change.`],
    [['L', 'f1'], `Failover: when the leader fails, the most up-to-date follower is promoted (automatically after a timeout, or by an operator) and clients are redirected to it.`]],
  sections: [
    ['Three architectures', TBL(['', 'Single-leader', 'Multi-leader', 'Leaderless'], [['Who accepts writes', 'One leader', 'Several leaders (often one per region)', 'Any replica'], ['Conflicts', 'None (one order)', 'Concurrent writes to the same row conflict', 'Resolved with versions, quorums, read repair'], ['Strength', 'Simple, consistent ordering', 'Local writes in every region, survives region loss', 'High availability, no failover'], ['Examples', 'PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB', 'Multi-region active–active setups, CouchDB', 'Cassandra, DynamoDB, Riak']])],
    ['Synchronous vs asynchronous', PP(`Synchronous replication waits for replicas, so a confirmed write survives the leader’s loss, but every write pays the replica’s round trip and stalls if the replica is down. Asynchronous replication is fast and tolerant, but a leader crash can lose the last few writes that hadn’t shipped. A common compromise is semi-synchronous: wait for one replica, the rest async.`)],
    ['Replication lag problems', TBL(['Anomaly', 'Example', 'Fix'], [['Read-your-writes', 'You post a comment, refresh, and it’s gone', 'Read your own data from the leader for a short time after writing, or track the write’s log position'], ['Monotonic reads', 'A comment appears, then disappears on the next refresh (different replicas)', 'Pin each user to one replica'], ['Consistent prefix', 'You see an answer before its question', 'Write causally related data to the same partition']])],
    ['Failover dangers', UL([`<b>Split brain:</b> the old leader comes back believing it is still leader, and two nodes accept writes. Use fencing (epoch numbers, shutting off the old node) and consensus-based leader election.`, `<b>Lost writes:</b> with async replication, promoting a lagging follower discards writes the old leader had acknowledged.`, `<b>Timeouts:</b> too short causes needless failovers during load spikes; too long means long outages.`])],
    ['Change data capture', PP(`The same replication log can feed other systems: search indexes, caches, analytics warehouses. Tools such as Debezium read the database log and publish each change as an event, which keeps derived systems in sync without dual writes from the application.`)]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('A user updates their profile, refreshes, and sees the old version. What is the likely cause?', ['The leader lost the write', 'The read went to an asynchronous follower that hadn’t applied the change yet', 'The index is missing', 'DNS caching'], 1, 'Replication lag breaks read-your-writes; route that user’s reads to the leader briefly.'),
    SQ('What does synchronous replication to one follower guarantee?', ['Reads are never stale', 'An acknowledged write exists on at least two machines', 'Writes are faster', 'No failover is ever needed'], 1, 'The leader confirms only after the follower has the write, so one machine failing can’t lose it.')]
});

SDX('sharding', { name: 'Sharding (partitioning)', cat: 'sddist',
  blurb: `When data or write traffic outgrows one machine, split it into shards, each holding a subset of the rows on its own server. The shard key decides which shard owns each row, and choosing it well is the most important decision in a sharded design.`,
  facts: [['Splits', 'Data and write load'], ['Key decision', 'The shard key'], ['Strategies', 'Hash, range, directory'], ['Hard parts', 'Hot spots, cross-shard queries']],
  dg: { w: 760, h: 330,
    n: [['app', 'App', 85, 165, 'svc'], ['rt', 'Shard router', 300, 165, 'lb', 'hash(user_id) mod 4'], ['s0', 'Shard 0', 560, 40, 'db', 'users …0'], ['s1', 'Shard 1', 560, 125, 'db', 'users …1'], ['s2', 'Shard 2', 560, 210, 'db', 'users …2'], ['s3', 'Shard 3', 560, 295, 'db', 'users …3']],
    e: [['app', 'rt', 'user 1017'], ['rt', 's1', 'hash → 1'], ['rt', 's0', '', 'd'], ['rt', 's2', '', 'd'], ['rt', 's3', '', 'd']] },
  cap: `Hash sharding: the router hashes the shard key to choose exactly one shard. Queries that include the key touch one shard; queries without it must ask all of them.`,
  walk: [[['app', 'rt', 'app>rt'], `A request for user 1017 reaches the routing layer: a library in the app, a proxy (like Vitess for MySQL), or the database itself.`],
    [['rt', 's1', 'rt>s1'], `hash(1017) mod 4 = 1, so only Shard 1 is touched. Each shard holds about a quarter of the data and handles about a quarter of the writes.`],
    [['rt', 's0', 's2', 's3', 'rt>s0', 'rt>s2', 'rt>s3'], `A query that doesn’t include user_id, such as “all orders over $1,000 today”, must be sent to every shard and the results merged (scatter–gather), which is slower and costlier.`],
    [['s0', 's1', 's2', 's3'], `Adding a fifth shard with mod N would move most keys. Consistent hashing or a fixed set of many logical partitions (say 1,024 mapped onto physical servers) moves only a small fraction.`]],
  sections: [
    ['Partitioning strategies', TBL(['Strategy', 'How', 'Good', 'Bad'], [['Hash', 'shard = hash(key) mod N, or consistent hashing', 'Even spread of data and load', 'Range queries hit every shard'], ['Range', 'Keys A–F on shard 1, G–M on shard 2…', 'Efficient range scans, easy splits', 'Hot spots (e.g. time-based keys: all new writes go to the last shard)'], ['Directory / lookup', 'A service maps each key or tenant to a shard', 'Full control, easy to move tenants', 'The directory is an extra dependency to scale and cache'], ['Geographic', 'By user region', 'Data near users, data residency laws', 'Uneven sizes, users who travel']])],
    ['Choosing a shard key', UL([`High cardinality: many distinct values, so data can spread.`, `Even access: no single value gets a large share of traffic.`, `Matches the main queries: the most common requests include the key, so they hit one shard.`, `Keeps data that changes together on the same shard, so transactions stay local (e.g. shard orders by customer_id, not order_id, if you query by customer).`]) +
      CO('Example:', `for a chat app, shard messages by conversation_id: every message in a conversation lives together, and loading a conversation is a single-shard range read.`)],
    ['Hot spots', PP(`A celebrity’s account or a viral product can overload one shard even with perfect hashing. Remedies: split the hot key (append a random suffix 0–9 to spread its writes over ten partitions and merge on read), cache its reads aggressively, or give it a dedicated shard.`)],
    ['The costs of sharding', UL([`Cross-shard joins and transactions are slow or unavailable; denormalize or do them in the application.`, `Secondary indexes are either local (each shard indexes its own data, so queries scatter) or global (partitioned by the indexed value, updated asynchronously).`, `Rebalancing moves data while serving traffic.`, `Operations multiply: backups, schema changes and monitoring per shard.`]) +
      PP(`Shard as late as you reasonably can; vertical scaling, read replicas and caching often go further than expected. Distributed SQL databases (Spanner, CockroachDB) and DynamoDB or Cassandra shard automatically.`)]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('Orders are sharded by order_id, but the main page lists a customer’s orders. What happens?', ['One shard is queried', 'Every shard must be queried for each page view', 'Writes become slower', 'Nothing changes'], 1, 'The query doesn’t include the shard key, so it scatters. Sharding by customer_id makes it a single-shard read.'),
    SQ('Why is a timestamp a poor range-shard key for an event log?', ['Timestamps aren’t unique', 'All new writes land on the newest range, creating a hot shard', 'Range sharding can’t sort', 'Timestamps are too large'], 1, 'Monotonic keys concentrate writes at the end of the key space.')]
});

SDX('consistenthash', { name: 'Consistent hashing', cat: 'sddist',
  blurb: `With hash mod N, changing N remaps almost every key. Consistent hashing places servers and keys on a ring; each key belongs to the next server clockwise, so adding or removing a server moves only about 1/N of the keys.`,
  facts: [['Keys moved on resize', '≈ 1/N (vs ≈ all with mod N)'], ['Lookup', 'O(log V) binary search'], ['Balance', 'Virtual nodes'], ['Used by', 'Dynamo, Cassandra, CDNs, load balancers']],
  dg: { w: 860, h: 390,
    c: [[320, 195, 150]],
    n: [['A', 'Server A', 320, 45, 'svc'], ['B', 'Server B', 416, 310, 'svc'], ['C', 'Server C', 190, 270, 'svc'], ['D', 'Server D', 470, 208, 'ext', 'new'],
      ['k1', 'key “cat”', 435, 98, 'box'], ['k2', 'key “dog”', 294, 343, 'box'], ['k3', 'key “eel”', 205, 98, 'box']],
    e: [['k1', 'B', 'clockwise', '', 'k1b'], ['k2', 'C', '', ''], ['k3', 'A', '', ''], ['k1', 'D', 'moves', 'd', 'k1d']],
    t: [['hash(key) and hash(server) share one ring', 610, 60, 'start'], ['each key → next server clockwise', 610, 80, 'start']] },
  cap: `Servers and keys hash onto the same ring; each key is stored on the first server clockwise from it. When Server D joins, only the keys between A and D move (to D); every other key stays put.`,
  walk: [[['A', 'B', 'C'], `Hash each server’s name onto a ring of positions (for example 0 … 2³² − 1). Here A, B and C land at three points.`],
    [['k1', 'k2', 'k3'], `Hash each key onto the same ring. To find a key’s owner, walk clockwise from the key to the first server.`],
    [['k1', 'B', 'k1b', 'k2', 'C', 'k2>C', 'k3', 'A', 'k3>A'], `“cat” belongs to B, “dog” to C, “eel” to A. Lookup is a binary search in the sorted list of server positions.`],
    [['D', 'k1', 'k1d'], `Add Server D between A and B. Only keys between A and D change owner: “cat” moves to D. Keys owned by A and C are untouched. With mod N, adding a fourth server would have moved about 75% of all keys.`],
    [['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], `Removing a server hands its keys to the next server clockwise, again moving only its share. Virtual nodes (each server at 100–200 ring positions) smooth out the uneven arc lengths.`]],
  sections: [
    ['The problem with mod N', PP(`If shard = hash(key) mod N, going from 4 to 5 servers changes the owner of about 4/5 of all keys. For a cache, that means nearly every lookup misses at once; for a database, nearly all data must move. Consistent hashing changes ownership only for keys in the affected arc: about 1/(N + 1) of them when adding one server.`)],
    ['Algorithm', OL([`Hash every server ID to a position on a ring of 2³² positions. Keep the positions in a sorted array.`, `To place key k, compute h = hash(k) and binary-search for the first server position ≥ h, wrapping around to the first position if none.`, `To add a server, insert its positions; only keys in the arcs just counter-clockwise of the new positions move.`, `For replication, store each key on the next R distinct servers clockwise.`])],
    ['Virtual nodes', PP(`With a few servers, random positions create very uneven arcs: one server might own 50% of the ring. Giving each physical server many virtual positions (say 150) averages the arcs out, so load is within a few percent of even. Virtual node counts can also be weighted: a server with twice the capacity gets twice the positions. When a server fails, its many small arcs are spread across all the others instead of dumping everything on one neighbor.`)],
    ['Alternatives', TBL(['Method', 'Idea', 'Notes'], [['Rendezvous (HRW) hashing', 'For each key, score every server with hash(key, server); pick the highest', 'Minimal movement, no ring; O(N) per lookup'], ['Jump consistent hash', 'A fast formula mapping a key to one of N buckets', 'No memory, perfect balance, but buckets can only be added or removed at the end'], ['Fixed logical partitions', 'Split into many partitions (e.g. 1,024); move whole partitions between servers', 'Used by Kafka, Elasticsearch, Riak, Couchbase']])],
    ['Where it is used', UL([`Partitioning in Amazon Dynamo, Cassandra and Riak.`, `Distributed caches (memcached client libraries), so adding a cache node doesn’t empty the cache.`, `Load balancers routing a user or URL to the same backend (Envoy’s ring hash and Maglev).`, `CDNs choosing which edge server in a location caches each object.`])]
  ],
  code: null,
  qs: [SQ('A cache cluster grows from 10 to 11 nodes. Roughly what fraction of keys move with consistent hashing?', ['≈ 1/11', '≈ 1/2', '≈ 10/11', 'All of them'], 0, 'Only keys in the new node’s arcs move: about 1/(N + 1).'),
    SQ('What problem do virtual nodes solve?', ['Hash collisions', 'Uneven arc sizes causing unbalanced load', 'Network partitions', 'Slow binary search'], 1, 'Many positions per server average out the randomness of a few positions.')]
});

SDX('blob', { name: 'Object (blob) storage', cat: 'sddata',
  blurb: `Photos, videos, backups and logs don’t belong in a database. Object storage (Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, Azure Blob) stores files of any size as objects in a flat namespace, with extreme durability and a simple HTTP API, at a low price per gigabyte.`,
  facts: [['Durability (S3)', '99.999999999%'], ['Object size', 'Up to 5 TB'], ['Access', 'HTTP PUT / GET'], ['Pattern', 'Presigned direct uploads']],
  dg: { w: 780, h: 330,
    n: [['c', 'Mobile app', 95, 140, 'client'], ['api', 'Upload API', 360, 50, 'svc'], ['s3', 'Object storage', 360, 250, 'store', 'photos/u42/a1b2.jpg'], ['q', 'Upload event', 640, 250, 'queue'], ['w', 'Thumbnailer', 640, 150, 'worker'], ['md', 'Metadata DB', 640, 50, 'db', 'owner, key, size'], ['cdn', 'CDN', 95, 290, 'cdn']],
    e: [['c', 'api', '1 · get upload URL'], ['c', 's3', '2 · PUT file'], ['s3', 'q', '3 · created'], ['q', 'w', '4 · resize'], ['w', 'md', '5 · metadata'], ['cdn', 's3', 'GET (cached)', 'd']] },
  cap: `Direct-to-storage uploads: the API hands out a short-lived presigned URL, the client uploads the bytes straight to object storage, and an event triggers processing. App servers never carry the file.`,
  walk: [[['c', 'api', 'c>api'], `The app asks the API to upload a photo. The API authenticates the user, picks an object key, and returns a presigned URL valid for a few minutes that allows exactly one PUT to that key.`],
    [['c', 's3', 'c>s3'], `The client uploads the file directly to object storage over HTTPS. Large files use multipart upload: parts in parallel, each retryable.`],
    [['s3', 'q', 'q', 'w', 's3>q', 'q>w'], `Storage emits an “object created” event to a queue; a worker generates thumbnails and other sizes, writing them back as new objects.`],
    [['w', 'md', 'w>md'], `The worker records metadata (owner, key, dimensions, status) in the database, which stores only the reference, never the bytes.`],
    [['cdn', 's3', 'cdn>s3'], `Downloads go through a CDN that caches objects; private files use signed, expiring URLs.`]],
  sections: [
    ['Why not the database or local disk?', UL([`Databases are expensive per gigabyte and large binary rows bloat backups, replication and caches.`, `Local disks disappear with the server and can’t be shared by a fleet of stateless servers.`, `Object storage scales to exabytes, replicates across facilities automatically, and costs a few cents per GB-month.`])],
    ['How object stores work', PP(`Objects are immutable blobs addressed by bucket and key, with metadata. Internally, a metadata service maps keys to data locations, and data is split across many disks using replication or erasure coding (for example, 10 data fragments plus 4 parity fragments: any 10 of 14 rebuild the object, at 1.4× storage instead of 3×). That is how S3 reaches eleven nines of durability.`)],
    ['Design patterns', UL([`<b>Presigned URLs</b> for uploads and private downloads, so bytes bypass your servers.`, `<b>Multipart and resumable uploads</b> for large files and flaky mobile networks.`, `<b>Content-addressed keys</b> (hash of the content) to deduplicate identical files.`, `<b>Storage classes and lifecycle rules:</b> move old data to infrequent-access or archive tiers (Glacier) automatically.`, `<b>Events</b> on upload to trigger processing pipelines.`])],
    ['Limits to know', UL([`Higher latency than a database (tens of milliseconds per request) and no partial updates: you rewrite whole objects.`, `Listing huge buckets is slow; keep your own index of keys in a database.`, `Request rates are high but per-prefix limited; spread keys across prefixes for extreme throughput.`])]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('Why hand the client a presigned URL instead of uploading through your API servers?', ['It encrypts the file', 'Large uploads bypass and don’t tie up your app servers, while access stays controlled and time-limited', 'It is required by HTTP', 'It makes files public'], 1, 'The URL grants one specific operation for a short time; the bytes go straight to storage.')]
});

SDX('searchindex', { name: 'Search & inverted indexes', cat: 'sddata',
  blurb: `Databases are poor at “find documents containing these words, ranked by relevance”. Search engines build an inverted index that maps each term to the documents containing it, then score matches. They are usually fed from the primary database and are eventually consistent with it.`,
  facts: [['Structure', 'term → posting list'], ['Ranking', 'BM25, plus signals'], ['Freshness', 'Near real-time (≈ 1 s)'], ['Engines', 'Elasticsearch, OpenSearch, Solr']],
  dg: { w: 800, h: 320,
    n: [['db', 'Products DB', 90, 70, 'db', 'source of truth'], ['cdc', 'Change stream', 300, 70, 'queue', 'CDC events'], ['ix', 'Indexer', 510, 70, 'worker', 'tokenize · stem'], ['si', 'Search index', 510, 240, 'db', 'term → doc IDs'], ['api', 'Search API', 280, 240, 'svc'], ['c', 'User', 85, 240, 'client']],
    e: [['db', 'cdc', 'row changed'], ['cdc', 'ix', ''], ['ix', 'si', 'update postings'], ['c', 'api', '“red shoes”'], ['api', 'si', 'match + rank'], ['api', 'db', 'hydrate details', 'd']] },
  cap: `The search index is a derived copy of the database, rebuilt from change events. Queries hit the index for matching IDs and scores, then optionally fetch full records from the database.`,
  walk: [[['db', 'cdc', 'db>cdc'], `When a product is created or edited, the change is published as an event (change data capture), rather than having the app write to two systems.`],
    [['cdc', 'ix', 'cdc>ix'], `The indexer analyzes text: splits it into tokens, lowercases, removes stop words, stems (“running” → “run”), and adds synonyms.`],
    [['ix', 'si', 'ix>si'], `It updates the inverted index: for each term, a sorted list of document IDs (a posting list), with positions and frequencies.`],
    [['c', 'api', 'api', 'si', 'c>api', 'api>si'], `A query “red shoes” is analyzed the same way, the posting lists for “red” and “shoe” are intersected, and matches are scored (BM25 for text relevance, plus popularity, recency or personalization).`],
    [['api', 'db', 'api>db'], `The top results’ IDs are returned, sometimes with details fetched from the database or stored in the index itself.`]],
  sections: [
    ['The inverted index', PP(`Like the index at the back of a book. Documents: 1 “red running shoes”, 2 “blue shoes”, 3 “red hat”. Index: red → [1, 3]; run → [1]; shoe → [1, 2]; blue → [2]; hat → [3]. The query red AND shoe intersects [1, 3] and [1, 2] → [1]. Sorted posting lists make intersections fast, and they compress well.`)],
    ['Ranking', UL([`<b>TF–IDF / BM25:</b> a term counts more if it appears often in the document and rarely across all documents.`, `<b>Field boosts:</b> a match in the title beats one in the description.`, `<b>Business signals:</b> popularity, ratings, recency, personalization, often combined by a learned ranking model.`, `<b>Semantic search:</b> vector embeddings and nearest-neighbor indexes (HNSW) find matches by meaning, not exact words; hybrid search combines both.`])],
    ['Scaling search', PP(`Indexes are split into shards (each a full mini-index over part of the documents) with replicas. A query goes to one copy of every shard; each returns its top results and a coordinator merges them. Add replicas for more query throughput and shards for more data. Index updates are batched and become visible after a refresh interval (about one second), so search is eventually consistent.`)],
    ['Design notes', UL([`Keep the database as the source of truth; the index can always be rebuilt from it.`, `Avoid dual writes from the application (one can fail); use change data capture or an outbox.`, `Typeahead (autocomplete) uses specialized prefix structures; see the typeahead case study.`])]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('Why is the search index usually updated from a change stream instead of written directly by the app alongside the DB?', ['It is faster for users', 'Dual writes can partially fail and diverge; a change stream gives one ordered source of updates', 'Search engines can’t accept writes', 'To save storage'], 1, 'Deriving the index from the database’s log keeps them consistent eventually, even after failures.')]
});

/* ---------- Messaging ---------- */
SDX('mq', { name: 'Message queues', cat: 'sdasync',
  blurb: `A message queue lets one service hand work to another without waiting for it. Producers enqueue messages; consumers process them at their own pace. Queues absorb traffic spikes, decouple services, and make slow work (emails, video encoding) asynchronous.`,
  facts: [['Delivery', 'Usually at least once'], ['Scaling', 'Add consumers'], ['Failures', 'Retries + dead-letter queue'], ['Examples', 'SQS, RabbitMQ, Kafka']],
  dg: { w: 780, h: 320,
    n: [['p', 'Checkout service', 105, 160, 'svc', 'producer'], ['q', 'Order queue', 360, 160, 'queue', 'buffered messages'], ['w1', 'Worker 1', 620, 55, 'worker'], ['w2', 'Worker 2', 620, 160, 'worker'], ['w3', 'Worker 3', 620, 265, 'worker'], ['dlq', 'Dead-letter queue', 360, 285, 'queue', 'after 5 failures']],
    e: [['p', 'q', 'enqueue order'], ['q', 'w1', 'deliver'], ['q', 'w2', 'deliver'], ['w3', 'q', 'ack (delete)', '', 'ack'], ['q', 'dlq', 'poison message', 'd']] },
  cap: `Competing consumers: any free worker takes the next message. A message is deleted only after a worker acknowledges it; messages that keep failing go to a dead-letter queue for inspection.`,
  walk: [[['p', 'q', 'p>q'], `Checkout saves the order and enqueues “send confirmation, charge card, reserve stock” work, then responds to the user immediately instead of waiting for all of it.`],
    [['q', 'w1', 'w2', 'q>w1', 'q>w2'], `Workers pull messages. While a worker processes one, it is invisible to others (a visibility timeout of, say, 30 s).`],
    [['w3', 'q', 'ack'], `When a worker finishes, it acknowledges and the message is deleted. If the worker crashes, the timeout expires and another worker gets the message again: at-least-once delivery.`],
    [['q', 'dlq', 'q>dlq'], `A message that fails five times (bad data, a bug) moves to the dead-letter queue so it stops blocking work; engineers inspect and replay it later.`],
    [['q', 'w1', 'w2', 'w3'], `During a sales spike the queue grows instead of the system falling over, and autoscaling adds workers based on queue depth.`]],
  sections: [
    ['Why queues', UL([`<b>Decoupling:</b> producers don’t need consumers to be up, or even to know who they are.`, `<b>Load leveling:</b> bursts are absorbed and processed at a steady rate.`, `<b>Responsiveness:</b> users get an answer without waiting for slow work.`, `<b>Reliability:</b> work survives crashes and is retried.`, `<b>Scaling:</b> add consumers to go faster.`])],
    ['Delivery guarantees', TBL(['Guarantee', 'Meaning', 'Cost'], [['At most once', 'Never redelivered; may be lost', 'Cheapest; fine for metrics'], ['At least once', 'Never lost; may be delivered twice', 'Consumers must be idempotent'], ['Exactly once', 'Effectively processed once', 'Only within a closed system (e.g. Kafka transactions) or with idempotent consumers plus deduplication']]) +
      PP(`In practice: at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing (dedupe by message ID, or design operations so repeating them is harmless).`)],
    ['Ordering', PP(`Standard queues don’t guarantee order across consumers. If order matters per entity (all events for order 123 in sequence), use FIFO queues with a group ID or a log partitioned by that ID, so one consumer handles each entity’s messages in order. Global ordering across everything limits throughput to one consumer.`)],
    ['Queue vs pub/sub vs log', TBL(['', 'Work queue', 'Pub/sub', 'Log (Kafka)'], [['Each message goes to', 'One consumer', 'Every subscriber', 'Every consumer group'], ['After consumption', 'Deleted', 'Deleted per subscriber', 'Retained (days or forever)'], ['Replay', 'No', 'No', 'Yes, from any offset'], ['Example', 'SQS, RabbitMQ', 'SNS, Redis Pub/Sub, Google Pub/Sub', 'Kafka, Kinesis, Pulsar']])],
    ['Pitfalls', UL([`<b>Backpressure:</b> a queue that grows forever is an outage in slow motion; alert on age of the oldest message and scale consumers.`, `<b>Poison messages:</b> always configure a dead-letter queue.`, `<b>Dual writes:</b> saving to the database and then enqueueing can fail halfway; use the transactional outbox pattern.`, `<b>Long processing:</b> extend visibility timeouts or the message will be redelivered while still being processed.`])]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('A worker charges a card, then crashes before acknowledging. What happens and what protects the customer?', ['The message is lost; nothing needed', 'The message is redelivered; the charge must be idempotent (e.g. keyed by order ID)', 'The queue rolls back the charge', 'The DLQ refunds it'], 1, 'At-least-once delivery means duplicates happen, so side effects need idempotency.')]
});

SDX('eventlog', { name: 'Event streaming & Kafka', cat: 'sdasync',
  blurb: `A distributed log such as Apache Kafka stores events durably in order, in partitions, and lets many independent consumer groups read them at their own pace and replay history. It is the backbone of event-driven architectures and real-time data pipelines.`,
  facts: [['Unit', 'Topic → partitions'], ['Order', 'Per partition'], ['Retention', 'Hours to forever'], ['Throughput', 'Millions of events/s per cluster']],
  dg: { w: 800, h: 360,
    n: [['p', 'Order service', 95, 180, 'svc', 'producer'], ['p0', 'Partition 0', 350, 75, 'queue', 'offsets 0…8,201'], ['p1', 'Partition 1', 350, 180, 'queue', 'offsets 0…7,944'], ['p2', 'Partition 2', 350, 285, 'queue', 'offsets 0…8,107'],
      ['e1', 'Email worker 1', 640, 60, 'worker'], ['e2', 'Email worker 2', 640, 145, 'worker'], ['an', 'Analytics', 640, 285, 'worker', 'reads all 3']],
    e: [['p', 'p0', 'key = order 17'], ['p', 'p1', ''], ['p', 'p2', ''], ['p0', 'e1', ''], ['p1', 'e2', ''], ['p2', 'e2', ''], ['p0', 'an', '', 'd'], ['p1', 'an', '', 'd'], ['p2', 'an', '', 'd']],
    z: [['Consumer group: email', 540, 18, 210, 175], ['Group: analytics', 540, 235, 210, 95]] },
  cap: `A topic with three partitions. Events with the same key always go to the same partition, so they stay in order. Each consumer group gets every event; within a group, each partition is read by exactly one consumer.`,
  walk: [[['p', 'p0', 'p>p0'], `The producer sends “order 17 paid” with key order-17. hash(key) picks partition 0, so every event for order 17 lands there in order.`],
    [['p0', 'p1', 'p2'], `Each partition is an append-only log on disk, replicated to (typically) 3 brokers. An event’s position is its offset. Events stay for the retention period whether or not anyone has read them.`],
    [['p0', 'p1', 'p2', 'e1', 'e2', 'p0>e1', 'p1>e2', 'p2>e2'], `The email group has two consumers sharing three partitions. Each consumer reads its partitions in order and periodically commits its offset. Adding a third consumer would rebalance to one partition each; a fourth would sit idle.`],
    [['p0', 'p1', 'p2', 'an', 'p0>an', 'p1>an', 'p2>an'], `The analytics group reads the same events completely independently, from its own offsets. A new team can start a group tomorrow and replay months of history.`]],
  sections: [
    ['Core concepts', TBL(['Term', 'Meaning'], [['Topic', 'A named stream of events (orders, clicks)'], ['Partition', 'An ordered, append-only log; the unit of parallelism and ordering'], ['Offset', 'An event’s position in its partition'], ['Broker', 'A server storing partitions; each partition has a leader and followers'], ['Consumer group', 'A set of consumers sharing the work; each group sees every event'], ['Retention', 'How long events are kept (time, size, or compacted to the latest value per key)']])],
    ['Why it is fast', UL([`Sequential disk writes and reads, which are fast even on spinning disks.`, `Batching and compression of many events per request.`, `Zero-copy transfer from the operating system’s page cache to the network.`, `Consumers track their own position, so brokers keep almost no per-consumer state.`])],
    ['Patterns it enables', UL([`<b>Event-driven services:</b> services react to events instead of calling each other synchronously.`, `<b>Event sourcing:</b> the log of events is the source of truth; current state is a fold over it.`, `<b>Change data capture:</b> database changes streamed to caches, search and warehouses.`, `<b>Stream processing:</b> windows, joins and aggregates over live events (Kafka Streams, Flink).`, `<b>Log compaction:</b> keep the latest event per key, turning a topic into a replayable table.`])],
    ['Design decisions', UL([`<b>Partition count</b> caps consumer parallelism within a group; choose it for peak throughput with room to grow.`, `<b>Partition key</b> decides ordering and balance; a skewed key creates a hot partition.`, `<b>Delivery semantics:</b> commit offsets after processing for at-least-once; use idempotent producers and transactions for exactly-once within Kafka.`, `<b>Replication:</b> acks=all with min.insync.replicas=2 for durability.`])]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('A topic has 6 partitions and a consumer group has 8 consumers. What happens?', ['All 8 share each partition', '6 consumers each get a partition; 2 sit idle', 'Kafka creates 2 more partitions', 'Messages are duplicated to all 8'], 1, 'Within a group, each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer, so parallelism is capped by partition count.'),
    SQ('How do you guarantee all events for one user are processed in order?', ['Use one partition for everything', 'Use the user ID as the message key', 'Use more consumers', 'Disable retention'], 1, 'Same key → same partition → ordered, while different users still spread across partitions.')]
});

SDX('batchstream', { name: 'Batch & stream processing', cat: 'sdasync',
  blurb: `Batch processing computes over large, bounded datasets periodically (yesterday’s logs); stream processing computes continuously over unbounded events as they arrive (fraud scores in milliseconds). Most data platforms use both.`,
  facts: [['Batch', 'High throughput, high latency'], ['Stream', 'Low latency, continuous'], ['Batch tools', 'Spark, MapReduce, SQL warehouses'], ['Stream tools', 'Flink, Kafka Streams, Spark Streaming']],
  dg: { w: 800, h: 330,
    n: [['ev', 'Click events', 90, 165, 'queue', 'Kafka'], ['sp', 'Stream processor', 350, 60, 'worker', 'Flink · 1-min windows'], ['lake', 'Data lake', 350, 270, 'store', 'S3 · Parquet'], ['bj', 'Batch job', 590, 270, 'worker', 'Spark · nightly'], ['rt', 'Serving DB', 590, 60, 'db', 'live counts'], ['dash', 'Dashboard', 740, 165, 'client']],
    e: [['ev', 'sp', 'continuous'], ['ev', 'lake', 'archive'], ['sp', 'rt', 'upsert'], ['lake', 'bj', 'scan day'], ['bj', 'rt', 'correct totals', 'd'], ['rt', 'dash', 'query']] },
  cap: `Events feed a stream processor for live numbers and an archive for batch jobs, which recompute exact results over complete data. Both write to the store the dashboard reads.`,
  walk: [[['ev', 'sp', 'ev>sp'], `Every click event flows into a stream processor that maintains running aggregates per minute window (views per page), with seconds of latency.`],
    [['sp', 'rt', 'sp>rt'], `Results are written continuously to a serving database, so dashboards show what is happening now.`],
    [['ev', 'lake', 'ev>lake'], `The same events are archived cheaply in columnar files in object storage.`],
    [['lake', 'bj', 'bj', 'rt', 'lake>bj', 'bj>rt'], `A nightly batch job scans the complete day, handling late and duplicate events exactly, and overwrites the day’s totals. This combination is the Lambda architecture; the Kappa architecture instead reprocesses by replaying the log through the same stream job.`]],
  sections: [
    ['Batch processing', PP(`Batch jobs read a large, fixed input, transform it and write an output, often on a schedule. MapReduce introduced the model: map each record to key–value pairs, shuffle so equal keys meet on one machine, reduce each group. Spark keeps intermediate data in memory and offers SQL and DataFrame APIs. Batch is simple to reason about: rerunning a job on the same input gives the same output, so bugs are fixed by fixing the code and rerunning.`)],
    ['Stream processing', UL([`<b>Windows:</b> tumbling (fixed, non-overlapping minutes), sliding, and session windows (activity separated by gaps).`, `<b>Event time vs processing time:</b> events arrive late and out of order; watermarks say “we have probably seen everything up to time T”.`, `<b>State:</b> counts and joins are kept in local state stores, checkpointed for recovery.`, `<b>Exactly-once results:</b> checkpoints plus transactional or idempotent sinks.`])],
    ['Choosing', TBL(['Need', 'Use'], [['Daily reports, model training, backfills', 'Batch'], ['Fraud detection, alerts, live dashboards, recommendations that react within seconds', 'Stream'], ['Both live and exact historical numbers', 'Stream for now, batch (or replay) for correctness']])],
    ['Related building blocks', UL([`<b>Data warehouse</b> (Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift): columnar SQL for analytics.`, `<b>Data lake / lakehouse:</b> open columnar files (Parquet) plus table formats (Iceberg, Delta) on object storage.`, `<b>ETL / ELT:</b> extracting from sources, transforming and loading; ELT loads raw data first and transforms in the warehouse.`, `<b>Orchestrators</b> (Airflow, Dagster) schedule and track batch pipelines.`])]
  ],
  qs: [SQ('Clicks can arrive minutes late from mobile devices. Which stream-processing concept handles that?', ['Round-robin partitions', 'Event-time windows with watermarks and allowed lateness', 'Larger batch sizes', 'Read replicas'], 1, 'Windows keyed on when the event happened, with watermarks, place late events in the right window.')]
});
